Z||000013||terryk||10-06-2000||10:11 PM||||Great Theogre, now you tell me. No wonder I kept flaring it when I'd increase the stanton feeder.....<P>Obviously, I was joking. The savings is not *that* great.<P>I think Dad (tm) might be thinking about his perpertual involvement in *your* car. At the same time, a good clean Fiero is not all that bad and actually pretty safe short of being an armored truck unless you're one of those people that can break a crowbar in a sandbox......<P>I have a 14yr old daughter and when she's old enough to drive, I'd let here drive a well kept Fiero. I would put strobes all over it though.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 10-06-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000014||2xsess||10-06-2000||10:26 PM||||Does anyone have a pic of a Fiero with the body panels off? I think one look at the space frame and "Dad" should be convinced of the safety issue. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Just a thought.<P>I've had 5 Fieros and never seen the coolant tubes cut or broken, I have seen them crushed but they still did their job.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||10-06-2000||11:32 PM||||Terry, You also need a framus spike. Can't do any of that without a spike.<P>Alisa... yeah, he means coolant pipes. It's easy to see if they are damaged. the pipes run behind the trim below the door. You can see them by just looking under the car.<P>If they are damaged there are a number of ways to repair them. some are easier than others. It's not a big deal. more annoying than difficult to fix.||||reg||1
Z||000016||EdsB52||10-06-2000||11:35 PM||||I've had 4 Fieros, totalled 2 of 'em, sold one, and still have my 87GT. The 2 I totalled were both 84's, so no great loss  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">. I've 4x4'd over stuff before and not damaged the coolant tubes that run under the baseboards (if that's what you're talking about), but I did crush them when I totalled 'em. My last wreck was where I slid over a median sidewards / backwards and met a light pole with my drivers door. I don't know how fast I was going when I contacted, but it caved the door in and I didn't receive a scratch. The other wreck (years ago), I was T-boned by a B210 in the passenger side. Hit so hard it broke the tie rod and tweeked the entire frame. It still ran and drove, just didn't take left turns very well  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">. The moral of this long ramble is that Fieros are not plastic cars as the average moron would have you believe, they're steel cars with plastic cosmetics. I've wrecked plenty of cars and bikes, and I would rate Fieros as one of the very safest. The old American ideology that states bigger is safer has been dispelled time and time again, but enough ignorance and misinformation is avialable to keep it alive. If your dad is worried about safety, ask him if he's heard of what is defined as the "the second collision." That's the premise for air-bags. The second collision is where the occupants hit the interior of the vehicle. With Cadys or pick-ups, it happens all the time. With sports cars that have divided seating, it's less frequent. Fieros are a pain in the ass to work on, but as far as safety, their a sinch. Good luck, Ed.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Seanh||10-06-2000||11:47 PM||||I will have some picts in Jan. when I strip my car and rebuild it. But yes, Fieros are very safe cars. A couple of years ago a couple of kids here were drunk and driving an 84 fiero. They pulled into a mall parking lot and were doing about 50mph throught it, somone was walking acoss the road, they swerved to miss them and the car went over the curb and they lost control and it went straight into the mall. It went through the cinder block wall and into the backroom of a store. Totaled the car, but they walked away with minor injuries. If this car can go through a cinder block wall at 50mph and the occupants walked away with minor injuries, its a darn safe car. Its also safe in the aspect that they handle great and are fairly difficult to loose control of despite the fact there is no power steering.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000018||frontal lobe||10-07-2000||12:04 AM||||Hey EdsB52,<P>It's nice to have one of the Knievel family on the forum!<P>Seriously, I'm glad you are ok after your multi-crashes.||||reg||1
Z||000019||SilverFieroV6||10-07-2000||02:11 AM||||hey Xtreme Fiero's<P>my first was in my fiero,  and now that i look back on it i am thankfull that i had the sunroof and her head was out it.<P><BR>Sam<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||tgowens||10-07-2000||03:13 AM||||<B>2xsess</B>, here is a picture of the space frame on one of our parts cars we are stripping.......  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-872f.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowens_sig.gif">                         <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> - Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 10-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||862M4inCA||10-07-2000||06:06 AM||||If safety is a concern with your getting the Fiero, there is some information I ran across in the web a few months back that showed the results for bother front driver and passenger in a head on collision...This vehicle rated #2 against other larger vehicles...This category also included much larger Cadilacs, Fords, Lincolns, etc. The only car to out permorm the Fiero in safety was the Volvo 240. Unfortuntely this test didn't have results for side impact but if you sit in the Fiero, you will notice that your quite some distance from the inner door panel, and even farther from the outer door panel where a collision would occur. I'll search for the site I got this safety information from and post a link if I find it. <P>Found something that might help. It's for the 84 model, but gives an idea. as well as results for other cars. <A HREF="http://www.mmit.com/ml320/crash.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mmit.com/ml320/crash.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by 862M4inCA (edited 10-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||Mattsfiero||10-07-2000||07:29 AM||||Fan Pipe....is he thinking about one of the recalls for the engine compartment fires? if so it is a simple hot air induction tube off of the cat and into the air intake (providing it is the Iron Duke 2.5 4cyl.) that is the only thing he should realy be thinking of. Heck I drive a 85 base coupe with some slight modifications  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>85 custom coupe<BR>Mattsfiero@excite.com<BR>www.anglefire.com/ma3/mattsfiero||||reg||1
Z||000023||Heisenhund||10-07-2000||08:10 AM||||Perhaps your Dad has heard from someone who was talking about the air cooling ducts that run from a fan under the trunk to the alternator and coil. In that case they always look cut. But I don't believe the 2M4's have these pipes.<P>Never heard anything bad about them myself. Although the first time I looked, I thought they were some modification or maybe part of a recall. <P>Heis<BR>86 GT 5 speed<BR>85 GT Auto<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000024||stsmithgt||10-07-2000||09:37 AM||||C'mon guys,  there ARE fan pipes on a Fiero!  No kidding.  How about those Pipes the fan in the trunk uses to cool the alternater and where the coil is?  Those could be called fan pipes, but as for them getting cut, it's more like you might get cut on the ends of those pipes.(if you sharpen them <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)  I'd rather polish them since they're alunimum <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  Well when ever I get my car back. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">????! I'm going through withdrawls without my GT. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>||||reg||4
