Z||000047||Tigger||10-09-2000||09:08 AM||||Well, hope you find and can get a Fiero.<P>The coolant pipes or what your dad calls fan pipes are about the least of concerns.  They are visible and easy to check just by looking under the car.  Your dad probably heard a horror story about how they can get crushed when a shop or lame owner jacks the car up on them thinking they are there to act like a roll cage.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Other than off roading or by some innocent curb jumping is pretty much how they can get crushed or cut.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tigger.gif">             <BR>    <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A>  <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000048||Gold-86SE||10-09-2000||11:14 AM||||<BR>Let me add my two cents.<P>Go to this address: <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/Athens/Ithaca/4878/crashtestpics.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/Athens/Ithaca/4878/crashtestpics.html</A> <P>it shows crash test pics of the NHTSA, National Highway Traffic Safety Assoc. - I think that is what is stands for.<P>Hope it helps.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.txconnect.com/home/rharris/signature-a.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000049||StuGood||10-09-2000||12:19 PM||||I would like to add, that Fieros are tough, very repairable cars.  I was plowed into by a 1970's Pontiac LeMans (what in the '70's we used to call a "midsize," -nowadays it would be termed a "tank") after I bought my first Fiero, a 1986 4-speed V6 SE.  It looked pretty bad, but I investigated and there was <B>no</B> damage to the frame or substructure.<P>Bought and installed some pieces myself; paid a dealership to do the rest.  Pocketed $350 of the insurance check.  After the repair, the car looked and acted <B>exactly</B> like new.   <P>Same car, about six months later.  I stupidly took a corner too fast, and encountered a patch of ice, which sent me into a curb, hard.  Wrecked the LR suspension badly - made me almost sick to look at it.  Main thing is, no one got hurt.<P>Bought a LR suspension from salvage, and did the work myself, except I had a dealer do a 4-wheel alignment.  Didn't know what I was doing, working in a poorly lighted garage in cold weather.  But it came out perfect.  Like new.  Not because I'm a talented suspension repair expert, but because the structure of the car is tough, it's overbuilt.   <P>Now, I'm certainly <B>not</B> encouraging you to get into accidents, but since they sometimes happen anyway, you might think about the fact that a Fiero is a very tough car - and therefore very repairable.  They sometimes just take a little patience.<P>Speaking of which, I know you're anxious to get a Fiero.  But don't let yourself be in too much of a hurry - for anything.  Rest assured, anything worth having, is worth waiting for. <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-09-2000).]||||reg||14
