Z||000060||DaRkLoRD||10-11-2000||06:35 PM||||My sister just turned 15 in September, and she already has about as much saved up for her first car (Fiero) as I do.. LOL<BR>(she wants either a red or metallic green 84 SE, with the aero notchback trim, wing, tinted windows etc.. and I'd like to replace the engine with a turbo 2.0L as well.)<P>I used to have more, but college bills really cut into my bank account...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>but I'm still on schedule for having my GT by April...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||12
Z||000061||2xsess||10-11-2000||06:39 PM||||If you stick with a 2M4 the insurance won't be too bad, I bought my first Fiero at 17, it was an 84 Base model. There wasn't much difference in insurance between it and my 1978 Olds Cutlass.<P>Good Luck<P>P.S. I'm not even going to give my comments about internet pediphiles! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000062||ur2biteme||10-12-2000||03:24 AM||||when i was first looking into fieros, i saw a fairly decent condition 84 SE.  it had about 174k on the clock and sounded strong as hell and the price was right ($800).  <P>i went to check insurance, i was 20 at the time, no tickets and no accidents on my record, and they wanted $146/mo for full coverage!  they were rating it as a high risk vehicle.  <P>this is also CA, so keep that in mind too.  some insurance companies still see them as high risk cars.  mostly the medium-to-high risk carriers that your easily recognizable carriers like AAA, Allstate, etc. go through when they won't cover you.  it shouldn't be that bad though.  they stick it to guys under 25, and especially california residents because most of us spend more time rubbernecking and talking on the cell phone than driving  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000063||DaRkLoRD||10-12-2000||02:41 PM||||It was about $3,000/year for me, for an 86 GT with a security system.. <BR>19 y/o male, G2, no tickets, on my dad's insurance plan. (full insurance.. collision, liability, theft, fire etc)<P>I'm 20 now, still got a G2.. still no tickets..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> so it won't be too much less than $3k.. that's $250/month.. it's also higher because I live in Toronto. It'd be lower if I still lived  in a small town.<P>The longer you've had a license, the lower the insurance is.. (unfortunately I didn't get my license until I was 18..) and insurance for female drivers is usually a bit less, and since a 4cyl Fiero isn't considered a sports car (a GT is, according to my insurance agency) it won't be as much as my insurance.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000064||Seanh||10-12-2000||05:42 PM||||My full coverage insurance runs around $3000 a year also. I am 18, no tickets or accidents. I thought mine was high, I have my stereo system insured for $3800 in addition to the full coverage on the car. Mines got a security system also, that lowered the rate some, but its still pretty expensive.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000065||StuGood||10-12-2000||06:27 PM||||On the insurance subject, here're a few (probably obvious) thoughts, plus some preaching slipped in at no extra charge...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Good Student Discount helps, so staying in school is good for many reasons.  Being female helps.  If Dad owns the car (but lets you drive it), he gets a multi-car discount.  If you work it out with Dad, so you pay him the additional cost of insurance, that helps both of you (daughter gets an insured car at a reasonalble rate; Dad gets multi-car discount).<P>It (almost) goes without saying that <B>if</B> daughter blows it, does something stupid and get into an acciddent, the deal's <B>off</B>, and it's <B>really</B> Dad's car     Therefore, daughter naturally becomes a cautions and extremely courteous, thoughtful driver.<P>Just a few thoughts, mind you.  Not trying to tell anyone what to do!    ||||reg||3
Z||000066||Raydar||10-12-2000||06:48 PM||||Something worth investigating... <BR>GTs are more expensive to insure. I'm not so sure  that applies to all of the V-6 cars, though.<BR>My 88 coupe and my Formula are within $50 a year of each other. Full coverage, collision, fire/theft, etc. The tag fees are also nearly identical. They make no distinction. It's either a coupe or a GT. And since a Formula is not a GT, then they are willing to call it a coupe, too. <BR>If you can find a 2M6 SE, it <I>may</I> be no more expensive to insure than a 2M4 (4 cylinder). <BR>My insurance is less than $1600 a year for <I>both</I> cars. (I'm an old fart. and I also don't have any tickets that they know of.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (soon to receive transplant.)<BR>88 "pretzeled" Formula <P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
