Z||000069||xfilesgirl1||10-13-2000||06:18 PM||||It's ok...don't worry....The way it's looking I don't think I'll be getting one. I will never be able to afford the insurance. $3000 a year is a little out of my range. I want one...but reasonably I don't think I'll be able to get it insured.  Maybe..gotta talk to my dad.<BR>thnx,<BR>Alisa<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by baldlobo:<BR><B> oh,sh!t; That's what I forgot to do sh!t, god just shoot me now please.<BR>Ps. as you can probably figured out by now that I forgot, sorry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000070||DaRkLoRD||10-13-2000||07:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by xfilesgirl1:<BR><B>It's ok...don't worry....The way it's looking I don't think I'll be getting one. I will never be able to afford the insurance. $3000 a year is a little out of my range. I want one...but reasonably I don't think I'll be able to get it insured.  Maybe..gotta talk to my dad.<BR>thnx,<BR>Alisa<BR> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's $3,000/year for me because I'm a male, (always higher for guys here....) it's a GT (classed as a sports car) and I don't have my full license yet. Only got a G2.. I have no restrictions on my driving, but if I get caught for speeding etc, I lose points.. and G2 class licenses don't have many points to begin with....<P>so your insurance will very likely be way lower than $3,000!<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000071||ur2biteme||10-14-2000||09:53 AM||||it's higher for guys everywhere you go.  i was quoted 145/mo for an 84 SE.  to some insurance companies, the name fiero is associated with high risk regardless of the engine in the bay.  <P>right now, my 87 costs me 35/mo for just liability and uninsured/underinsured motorist coverage.  if you can get away with it, just remember, some locations you only need liability insurance to be legal for the road.  <P>in all likelyhood though, you probably could find full coverage on any bone stock fiero (probably even a 6 cyl) for under 2000 or 1500 a year.  if i remember right, i think full coverage right now for me on my car would be about 90/mo.  <P>what you really need to do is go ahead and get insurance quotes.  they may change, and/or may not be completely accurate, but it should at least give you an idea.<P>dont give up hope yet.  you've just got more homework to do!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000072||ur2biteme||10-14-2000||09:55 AM||||oh yeah.  the 145/mo quote was from 2 years ago before i even found the car i'm driving.  ||||reg||1
Z||000073||Vlad||10-14-2000||12:09 PM||||It looks like I got a cheap coverage.<P>Vancouver, Canada<BR>male 26 years old<BR>Fiero GT V6 85<BR>full coverage<BR>--------------------<P>145 Can $ / month<P>BTW, this is ICBC. The only one company licensed to insure in BC.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000074||Seanh||10-14-2000||12:46 PM||||Yeah, most of the prices we said were for full coverage. It will be alot lower for Lia. only. I think you would be legal with it there, at least it should be. But then if you get in an accident you cause, say by by to your car unless you want to foot the bill to have it fixed or do it yourself. Somthing I hate about insurance here, dont know about any where else, if your hit or hit somone and your backing up, it's always your falut. Somone locally here (not a fiero) was backing out of thier drive way and someone hit him that was going 50 on a 25 residential street, but nope, it was his fault because he was backing up. I think things like that defeat the purpose of having insurance. With as high as insurance prices are now for me, I would never consider sending in a claim unless I was going to junk the car, its not worth it.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000075||Babygirl||10-14-2000||02:28 PM||||You guys should be ashamed of yourselves.  This young woman is 15 years old and you are making sexual references to her?  Do you think that this is helping her dad make a decision to buy her this car?  <P>Another thing,<BR>if you had a question about something that only most women know about would you expect to made the butt of a joke? Do you think this would encourage you to proceed with wanting to learn?<P>At a young age, she is very excited about this car and she gets made fun of by a bunch of Feiro Geeks.  It is really funny too, if you saw her in real life AND she was of age and you wanted to ask her out, she would probably turn your sorry ass down.<P>Intelligence is obviously not some of these guys' strong point.  Who the hell do you think you are anyways.  I bet it makes theogre feel really good to make a little girl feel insignificant and stupid eh?<P>Anyways, xfilesgirl1,  I wouldn't recommend buying a Fiero for your first car, there are a couple of reasons.<P>1.  This car is high maintenance.  That means when it breaks you have to wait for dad to fix it.  If he goes out of town a lot then you don't get to drive your car.<P>2.  Parts are generally expensive.  This car is older and has a tendency to break, you will have to buy parts.  Do you want your dad to dish out cash for a new EGR valve or for Friday night movies.<P>3.  They usually have exhaust leaks.  Every Fiero I have ever driven has an exhaust leak.  This means that you will smell exhaust in your car and when you get out of your car you will smell like exhaust.  Not good for impressing friends.<P>4.  You can only fit one person in your car.  I remember some of my best times in high school were piling people into my car and heading out.  More people is equal to more fun.<P>The only thing that could be of benefit to you by buying this car is that it looks cool.  You probably aren't going to appreciate the "on-rails" handling as you are probably not a precision driver yet.  You will be though.<P>A Honda CRX might be cool, or if you really<BR>want a reliable car that is still a two seater get a Toyota MR2, the older ones are comparable to Fiero's in price and are extremely quick and agile.  In fact today I have retired my Fiero and am going to buy an MR2.<P>Good Luck,<BR>BabyGirl's Husband<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000076||2xsess||10-14-2000||03:09 PM||||Listen Babygirls Husband, I've sat here and watch you slander the Fiero long enough. You ignorance is only equal to your obvious lack of consideration for others. Have you ever heard if you don't have anything nice to say don't say anything???? Hint- shut the heck up!!! How many Fieros have you actually driven? You remind me of the stupid mag authors who write up misinforming articles that cut down our cars. If you can't contain yourself than <B>GET OUT</B>. I hope you sell your Fiero soon so we won't have to hear from you any longer. Go buy your MR2 and see how  much the parts are for that little tin box. <P>xfilesgirl, I had an 88GT,84&84SE that I put numerous miles on. I never smelt exhaust inside the car. My car never caught on fire.<P>As a matter of fact I put almost 60,000 miles on my 88GT in south FL. I never had to do any of things that the previous post described, I'm not saying that every Fiero will do this but, I had a '92 Prelude that at 70,000 the distributor destroyed itself, that cost me $300, then the alternator went followed by the battery. I paid $13000 for that car and yes I liked the car and still did when I traded it in. <P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||8
Z||000077||2xsess||10-14-2000||03:10 PM||||Listen Babygirls Husband, I've sat here and watch you slander the Fiero long enough. You ignorance is only equal to your obvious lack of consideration for others. Have you ever heard if you don't have anything nice to say don't say anything???? <B>Hint- shut the heck up!!!</B> How many Fieros have you actually driven? You remind me of the stupid mag authors who write up misinforming articles that cut down our cars. If you can't contain yourself then <B>GET OUT</B>. I hope you sell your Fiero soon so we won't have to hear from you any longer. Go buy your MR2 and see how  much the parts are for that little tin box. <P>xfilesgirl, I had an 88GT,86GT,and 84&84SE that I put numerous miles on. I never smelt exhaust inside the car. Nor do I smell it in my 85GT. My car never caught on fire.<P>As a matter of fact I put almost 60,000 miles on my 88GT in south FL. I never had to do any of things that the previous post described, I'm not saying that every Fiero will do this but, I had a '92 Prelude that at 70,000 the distributor destroyed itself, that cost me $300, then the timing belt broke destroying the head, that cost me almost $2000, then the alternator went followed by the battery. I paid $13000 for that car and yes I liked the car and still did when I traded it in. <P><BR>In my opinion: only people who let their cars outsmart them have to insult other peoples intelligence!!!<BR>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT<P>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 10-14-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 10-14-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000078||Patrick||10-14-2000||03:33 PM||||<BR>One of the more bizzare facts evident in this thread is that there's a fella who continues to post under the name of <B><I>Babygirl</I></B>! So what if he's married to her? I mean really, it doesn't take a huge effort for anyone to register under their own username. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000079||862M4inCA||10-14-2000||03:35 PM||||In responce to all the slandering of the reliability of this car let me tell you a little about the history behind mine. I bought mine back in April of last year (paid $500) from a VERY good friend (still friends  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">). At the time I was a delivery driver for a pizza company, and used that car on a DAILY basis to deliver (I know it a sin to use a Fiero to deliver pizza, but what can ya do when you NEED the job and that's your only car). I NEVER had it break on me once during the year I was delivering in it, and that was after racking up 30K on an already tired car! As for the insurance deal, I have full coverage in CA from Allstate with one accident, i'm a 23 y/o male and I'm not married, AND they also know I use the car to deliver in, and still only pay about $140/mo. I'm sure when you get your insurance quote, either at that time they're inform you it's a "high performance car" or shortly after starting your insurance, but personally, it's cheaper than getting coverage for a lot of these little Honda cars, or Toyotas. I don't care how much you slam on the reliability of the Fiero, but I'd put the reliability WAY above that of the Datsun I had before, and that car run up to over 300K and STILL didn't burn/leak oil! BTW, I was paying $70/mo. for JUST liability on the Datsun (a hatchback B210 grocery-getter!!!) As for price of parts, I personally don't think they're that outrageous, especially seeing the bill for a shortblock my parents had replaced in their '92 Isuzu Impulse that played to the tune of $2700. I rather liked the quote of $699 I got when I called to see what one would run for mine.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Best suggestion to you Alisa is call around for insurance quotes, as well as basic parts (starter, alternator, plugs, wires, maintainence stuff really) and see how happy you are with the quotes. I'm sure most of the basic stuff is cheap enough that you might actually be surprised. Blake||||reg||1
Z||000080||mrfiero||10-14-2000||04:21 PM||||Nothing wrong with using a Fiero as a delivery vehicle.  I delivered pizzas in my '88 GT when I first bought it in 1990.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>As for the reliability issue......I have had very few problems with any of the Fieros I've owned (well over 2 dozen!).  My first Fiero was the same '88 GT mentioned above.....I bought it with 25K miles and drove it until it was totalled in an accident at 114K miles.<P>My next Fiero was an '88 Formula.....it had 88K miles on it and I put about 30K miles om it before selling it.  <P>I then bought another '88 Formula with 112K miles.  I sold it to a friend a year later with 130K miles (he drove it another 10K miles before it was totalled by a drunk driver).   <P>I then bought an '85 coupe with 160K miles....I drove it for another 10K miles before the engine let go.<P>I bought another '88 GT in May of this year with 114K miles on it......it now has 135K miles and I didn't drive it for 3 months!  (that's 21K miles in 3 months of driving).<P>Bottom line is the Fiero is a very reliable  car, but you get what you pay for.  If you're looking at $500 Fieros you're going to have problems.  Pay the extra $$ upfront for a lower mileage, great running Fiero and you'll be happier.<P>I generally pay about $2-$4000 for my Fieros (the ones I plan on driving)......none of them ever needed anything other than a tune-up after buying it.  In the more than 300K miles I've put on my Fieros  I've only been stranded a handful of times (other than running out of gas).....most were ignition related (pooped out coil/ignition module) and on was a shredded fan belt.  In the 24+ Fieros I've owned I've only lost 2 engines.....both had well over 100K miles so it wasn't a terrible shock (I got my money's worth).<P><BR>I've been T-boned, spun on the ice, hit a retaining wall, been smacked in the fenders twice and rear-ended once with no injuries whatsoever and, with the exception of the T-bone, was able to drive off after all of them.  The front & rear suspension were completely thrashed when I was t-boned and the car just couldn't drive a straight line.    Needless to say, these are very safe vehicles.  I have been using a Fiero as a daily driver for almost 11 years.....I have only owned 1 other vehicle and that was an S-10 truck (which I later sold to buy another Fiero!).<P>That's enough ranting for me....I won't even touch the other issues in this thread!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    <P><BR>BTW.....I pay less than $600/year for liability insurance.....I don't feel that full coverage is worth it, but then again.....I have enough parts to build a Fiero from scratch!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <BR> ||||reg||10
